doubts.body:
Our DSA lab evaluation is on Friday and my BST code is suddenly super slow. I am inserting the numbers 1, 2, 3, ... 1000 in order and the whole thing takes as long as a linked list. When I insert random numbers it is fast. Sir said in class that BST insert is O(log n) so I am confused.

This is my insert:

```java
Node insert(Node root, int key) {
    if (root == null) return new Node(key);
    if (key < root.key) root.left = insert(root.left, key);
    else root.right = insert(root.right, key);
    return root;
}
```

Is my code wrong, or is the O(log n) claim only true sometimes? How do I fix it?

answers.body:
Fast cannot jump over slow, because inside the cycle the gap between them changes by exactly one node per step.

Once both pointers are inside the cycle, say fast is `d` nodes behind slow along the cycle. In each step slow moves 1 and fast moves 2, so the gap shrinks by 1: d, d-1, ..., 1, 0. When it hits 0 they are on the same node. A gap can never go from 1 straight to -1, so there is no "swapping places" case. If fast moved 3 steps per round it could skip over slow, which is why 2 is the right choice.

Finding the start of the cycle:
- Let `a` be the distance from the head to the cycle start, and `b` the distance from the cycle start to the meeting point, and `L` the cycle length.
- Slow travelled a + b, fast travelled 2(a + b). Their difference is a whole number of laps: a + b = kL.
- So a = kL - b, meaning that walking `a` steps from the meeting point lands on the cycle start.

```java
ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
    slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow == fast) {
        ListNode p = head;
        while (p != slow) { p = p.next; slow = slow.next; }
        return p;           // start of the cycle
    }
}
return null;                // no cycle
```

doubts.body:
I wrote binary search for the lab and it works on most inputs but hangs on some, for example searching 8 in [2, 8]. I used low = mid in the else branch because I thought we should keep mid in the range. Here is the loop:

```c
int low = 0, high = n - 1;
while (low < high) {
    int mid = (low + high) / 2;
    if (a[mid] < key) low = mid;
    else high = mid;
}
```

Why does it hang? Also a friend told me (low + high) / 2 can be a bug in some cases, is that true?

answers.body:
With `low = mid`, the range may not shrink. Take [2, 8] with low = 0, high = 1. Then mid = (0 + 1) / 2 = 0. If a[0] < key you set low = mid = 0, which is the same as before. The loop never ends because low and high never change.

Fix it by always moving past mid, since you have already compared it:

```c
int low = 0, high = n - 1;
while (low <= high) {
    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == key) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < key) low = mid + 1;
    else high = mid - 1;
}
return -1;
```

Every iteration now removes at least one element, so it must terminate.

About your friend's point: (low + high) can overflow when both are large ints (above about 1 billion), giving a negative mid. `low + (high - low) / 2` never overflows. This famous bug was in the JDK's binary search for years, so using the safe form is a good habit.

DFS finds a path, but not necessarily the shortest one. BFS finds the shortest path (fewest edges) because of the order in which it visits nodes.

Example: edges A-B, B-C, C-D, A-D. Shortest path from A to D is the direct edge, length 1. If DFS goes A -> B first, it continues B -> C -> D and reaches D with length 3. It stops there and never looks at the direct edge.

BFS uses a queue and visits all nodes at distance 1, then all nodes at distance 2, and so on. The first time it reaches a node is guaranteed to be through the smallest number of edges.

To recover the path, store the parent of each node when you first discover it, then walk back from the target:

```java
int[] parent = new int[n];
Arrays.fill(parent, -1);
Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
q.add(src); parent[src] = src;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    int u = q.poll();
    for (int v : adj.get(u))
        if (parent[v] == -1) { parent[v] = u; q.add(v); }
}
// path: start at dst, follow parent[] until src, then reverse
```

Complexity is O(V + E). For weighted graphs BFS no longer works and you need Dijkstra.

doubts.body:
I wrote the recursive solution for the "number of ways to climb n stairs taking 1 or 2 steps" problem:

```java
int ways(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2);
}
```

It works for small n but for n = 45 it takes forever. A senior told me to add memoization and it will become O(n). I added a HashMap but I am not sure I did it right, and I do not see why the time drops so much. How many calls are we saving?

answers.body:
The plain recursion recomputes the same subproblems again and again. ways(5) calls ways(4) and ways(3), and ways(4) calls ways(3) again. The call tree roughly doubles at each level, so the number of calls grows like 2^n, and for n = 45 that is billions of calls.

There are only n + 1 distinct subproblems (ways(0) to ways(n)). Memoization stores each result the first time you compute it, and later calls just look it up:

```java
long[] memo = new long[n + 1];
long ways(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];
    return memo[n] = ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2);
}
```

Each subproblem is computed once with O(1) extra work, so the total is O(n) time and O(n) space. For n = 45 that is about 90 calls instead of billions.

Use `long` as well: ways(45) is 1,836,311,903, close to the int limit.

doubts.body:
For the OS lab we have to implement bounded buffer producer-consumer using semaphores. I used three semaphores: mutex = 1, empty = N and full = 0. My program works for a few items and then both threads just hang forever. Sir said in class that the order of wait calls matters but I did not catch why.

This is my producer loop:

```c
while (1) {
    item = produce();
    wait(mutex);
    wait(empty);
    buffer[in] = item;
    in = (in + 1) % N;
    signal(mutex);
    signal(full);
}
```

The consumer does wait(full), wait(mutex), takes the item, then signal(mutex), signal(empty). Where is the deadlock?

answers.body:
The deadlock is in the order of the two wait calls in the producer. You take the mutex first and then wait for an empty slot.

Walk through what happens when the buffer is full (empty = 0):
1. The producer does wait(mutex) and gets the lock.
2. It does wait(empty) and blocks, because there is no free slot. It is still holding the mutex.
3. The consumer wants to remove an item. It does wait(full), which succeeds, then wait(mutex), which blocks because the producer holds it.
4. The producer waits for the consumer to free a slot, and the consumer waits for the producer to release the mutex. Nobody can move. Deadlock.

The rule: wait on the counting semaphore (the resource) first, and take the mutex last and hold it for the shortest time.

```c
// producer
wait(empty);      // is there a free slot?
wait(mutex);      // now lock the buffer
buffer[in] = item; in = (in + 1) % N;
signal(mutex);
signal(full);

// consumer
wait(full);
wait(mutex);
item = buffer[out]; out = (out + 1) % N;
signal(mutex);
signal(empty);
```

The order of the signal calls does not cause deadlock, but the wait order must be empty then mutex for the producer and full then mutex for the consumer.

doubts.body:
For my Java lab I am removing all even numbers from an ArrayList while looping:

```java
List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));
for (Integer n : nums) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) nums.remove(n);
}
```

This throws ConcurrentModificationException. There are no threads in my program, so I do not understand what is concurrent here. Strangely, when I tried the list [1, 2, 3, 4] it did not throw at all. What is happening and what is the correct way to remove elements while iterating?

answers.body:
"Concurrent" here just means modification of the list while an iterator is still using it; threads are not needed.

The for-each loop is compiled into an Iterator. ArrayList keeps a counter `modCount` that increases on every structural change (add or remove). The iterator remembers the modCount when it was created, and on every call to `next()` it checks that the counter is unchanged. If you call `nums.remove(...)` directly, modCount changes, and the next `next()` throws ConcurrentModificationException. This behaviour is called fail-fast.

Why did [1, 2, 3, 4] not throw? Removing 3's neighbour at the second-to-last position makes `hasNext()` return false before `next()` is called again, so the check never runs. It is a quirk, not a guarantee, so never depend on it.

Correct ways:

```java
// 1. removeIf (Java 8+), the cleanest
nums.removeIf(n -> n % 2 == 0);

// 2. Iterator.remove(), which updates modCount in a way the iterator knows
Iterator<Integer> it = nums.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next() % 2 == 0) it.remove();
}

// 3. Build a new list with a stream
List<Integer> odd = nums.stream().filter(n -> n % 2 != 0).toList();
```

Note: `nums.remove(n)` with an `Integer` removes by object, but `nums.remove(int)` removes by index, so be careful with overloads.

doubts.body:
I have this code and I expected the output "Animal sound" because the variable type is Animal:

```java
class Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Animal sound"); } }
class Dog extends Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); } }

Animal a = new Dog();
a.sound();
```

It prints "Bark". Why does Java call the Dog method? What is the difference between overriding and overloading, and does the same thing happen with variables?

Our teacher asked this in the viva: what is printed by this code?

```java
static int test() {
    try {
        return 1;
    } finally {
        System.out.println("finally");
    }
}
```

I said it will not print because the method already returned. I think I was wrong. What exactly happens, and what if finally also has a return statement? Are there cases where finally does not run at all?

answers.body:
"finally" is printed, and then the method returns 1. The finally block runs even when try returns. The sequence is: the return value (1) is evaluated and saved, then the finally block executes, then the saved value is returned.

If finally modifies a primitive variable that was returned, it does not change the returned value, because the value was already copied:

```java
static int f() {
    int x = 1;
    try { return x; }
    finally { x = 2; }    // still returns 1
}
```

If finally itself has a return, it overrides the one from try and also swallows any exception that was in flight:

```java
static int g() {
    try { return 1; }
    finally { return 2; }  // returns 2; avoid this, it hides bugs
}
```

Finally does NOT run when: the JVM exits before reaching it (System.exit(), a fatal crash, power loss), the thread is killed, or the try block never starts executing (for example an infinite loop before it). Apart from those, it always runs, which is why it is used to close resources. For resources, prefer try-with-resources, which closes automatically.

The wrong total comes from `count++` not being atomic. It is three steps (read, add, write). Two threads can read the same value and both write back value + 1, losing an increment.

Synchronized method: locks on `this` (or the Class object for a static method) for the whole method body.

```java
synchronized void increment() { count++; }
```

Synchronized block: locks on an object you choose, only for the lines inside the block.

```java
private final Object lock = new Object();
void increment() {
    // other work that needs no lock can run here
    synchronized (lock) { count++; }
}
```

Which to use:
- Method form is simplest and fine when the entire method is the critical section.
- Block form keeps the critical section small (better throughput when the method also does slow work), and lets you lock on a private object so outside code cannot interfere by locking on your object.

The performance difference is mostly about how long the lock is held, not the keyword.

For a plain counter the best option is `AtomicInteger`: `count.incrementAndGet()`. It uses a hardware compare-and-swap instruction and needs no lock, so it is both simpler and faster under contention. Use synchronized when several variables must be updated together as one unit.
